ta. The proposed methods rely on a variety of techniques, including manifold alignment (Welch et al., 2017; Amodio and Krishnaswamy, 2018; Liu et al., 2019), matrix factorization (Duren et al., 2018), correlation analysis (Barkas et al., 2019; Stuart et al., 2019), coupled autoencoders (Yang and Uhler, 2019), optimal transport (Cao et al., 2022), regression analysis (Yuan and Duren, 2022), and semisupervised learning (Lin et al., 2022). Implicitly, these methods presume the existence of a *shared* latent space where the different modalities either completely align or at least overlap. However, to the best of our knowledge, none of these methods have rigorous *identifiability* guarantees, i.e., the methods are not guaranteed to recover a correct domain translation mapping even for infinite data. Our work advances the theoretical understanding of multi-domain integration by providing identifiability guarantees on recovering the shared latent space.\n\n**Group Independent Component Analysis.** The primary tool that we use for identifiability is linear independent component analysis (ICA) (Comon, 1994; Eriksson and Koivunen, 2004). Many works extend ICA to the multi-domain setting. These methods primarily come from computational neuroscience, where different domains correspond to different subjects or studies. However, to the best of our knowledge, all prior works require pairing between samples. These works can be categorized based on whether the samples are assumed to be voxels (Calhoun et al., 2001; Esposito et al., 2005), time points (Svensén et al., 2002; Varoquaux et al., 2009; Hyvärinen and Ramkumar, 2013), or either (Beckmann and Smith, 2005; Sui et al., 2009). For reviews, see Calhoun et al. (2009) and Chabriel et al. (2014). Related are methods for *independent vector analysis* (Kim et al., 2006; Anderson et al., 2014; Bhinge et al., 2019) and multiset canonical correlation analysis (Nielsen, 2002; Li et al., 2011; Klami et al., 2014), which allow the latent variables to take on different values in each domain but still require sample pairing. Most of the mentioned methods lack identifiability guarantees, only newer work (Richard et al., 2021) provides sufficient conditions for identifiability. Furthermore, all mentioned methods assume that every latent variable is shared across all domains, while our setup allows for shared and domain-specific latent variables. Some methods, e.g., Lukic et al. (2002), Maneshi et al. (2016), and Pandeva and Forré (2023), permit both shared and domain-specific components, but only consider the paired setting. In this paper, we extend these results to the *unpaired* setting.\n\n**Latent Structure Discovery.** Learning causal structure between latent variables has a long history, e.g., in measurement models (Silva et al., 2006). One recent line of work studies the problem under the assumption of access to interventional data (e.g., Liu et al., 2022; Squires et al., 2023). In particular, Squires et al. (2023) show that the latent graph is identifiable if the interventions are sufficiently diverse. Another line of work, closer to ours and not based on interventional data, shows that the graph is identified under certain sparsity assumptions on the mixing functions (Xie et al., 2020; Chen et al., 2022; Xie et al., 2022; Huang et al., 2022). However, these methods are not suitable in our setup since they require paired data in a single domain. One cannot apply them in each domain separately since it would be unclear how to combine the multiple latent causal graphs, that is, which of the latent variables are shared. In this work, we lay out sparsity assumptions on the mixing\n\n3  \n34467  \nhttps://doi.org/10.52202/075280-1496--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCausal & Geometric Representation Learning · Issue #24 · inoue0426/Paper-reading · GitHub (https://github.com/inoue0426/Paper-reading/issues/24)\nciteturn214search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * Unpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning\n\n# Causal & Geometric Representation Learning #24\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nOpen\n\nOpen\n\nCausal & Geometric Representation Learning#24\n\nCopy link\n\n## Description\n\ninoue0426\n\nopened on Oct 16, 2025\n\nIssue body actions\n\n  * Unpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning\n    * NeurIPS 2023\n    * MIT, Broad, TUM\n    * This paper proves that shared latent causal variables can be uniquely identified from unpaired multi-domain data.　It provides theoretical identifiability conditions and a linear method for recovering the shared causal graph.\n\n  * The Latent Space Hypothesis\n    * Arxiv\n    * Oxford\n    * This proposes that all medical data—genomic, imaging, physiological, and textual—are projections of a shared latent physiological space. It presents a conceptual framework that unifies multimodal medical foundation models via a latent space, linking biological hierarchy, interpretability, and causal reasoning.\n\n  * Score-based Causal Representation Learning: Linear and General Transformations\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/unpaired-multi-domain-causal-representation-learning)\nciteturn214search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Unpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning ...       title = {{Unpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning}},\n\n# Unpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning\n\nNils Sturma, Chandler Squires, Mathias Drton, Caroline Uhler\n\nNeurIPS 2023 5\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn many scientific fields, particularly single-cell biology, researchers face a significant data integration challenge: the inability to measure multiple modalities on the same sample simultaneously. Because many measurement techniques are destructive, a single cell might be sequenced for its DNA or imaged for its morphology, but rarely both. This results in unpaired data, where we possess the marginal distributions of different domains (e.g., imaging and sequencing) but lack a direct correspondence between individual samples.\n\nThis paper introduces a principled framework to bridge this gap by learning a shared causal representation. The goal is to identify a set of latent variables and their directed cause-and-effect relationships that explain the observations across all domains, even when those observations are never seen together.\n\n## The Challenge of Identifiability\n\nThe core of this research addresses the \"identifiability\" problem: determining the mathematical conditions under which the learned causal model is guaranteed to be the unique structure that produced the observed data. Traditional representation learning often assumes latent variables are independent, but real-world factors (like biological pathways) are typically linked. By moving beyond independence to a causal framework, the authors aim to recover the true underlying mechanisms of the system.\n\n## Methodology\n\nThe authors propose a two-step approach to recover the causal structure from unpaired marginals:\n\n### 1. Identifying the Joint Distribution via \"Fingerprints\"\n\nTo match latent variables across domains without paired samples, the framework leverages Linear Independent Component Analysis (ICA). The method assumes that the \"error\" or noise distributions of the latent variables are non-Gaussian. These unique distributions act as statistical \"fingerprints.\" If a specific latent variable in the imaging domain and a latent variable in the sequencing domain share the same non-Gaussian fingerprint (e.g., a specific Gumbel distribution), the algorithm can infer they represent the same underlying factor.\n\n### 2. Identifying the Causal Graph\n\nOnce the variables are matched across domains, the framework must determine the directed edges (the causal graph) between them. The authors introduce the concept of \"partial pure children\"—observed variables influenced by exactly one shared latent variable, though they may also be influenced by domain-specific noise. By performing rank analysis on sub-matrices of the \"overall mixing matrix,\" the framework can reconstruct the causal ordering and the specific edges of the latent graph.\n\n## Experimental Validation\n\nThe framework was evaluated using 1,000 simulated causal models to provide a proof of concept for its theoretical claims. The simulations involved:\n\n  * Varying Complexity: Models with 3 and 5 shared latent nodes.\n  * Multi-Domain Scaling: Setups involving 2, 3, and 4 domains with sample sizes ranging from 1,000 to 25,000 per domain.\n  * Distributional Variety: Testing against various non-Gaussian distributions including Beta, Gumbel, LogNormal, and Weibull.\n\n### Key Results\n\n  * Consistency: Estimation errors for both the mixing matrix and the causal graph decreased consistently as sample sizes increased.\n  * The Power of Consensus: The probability of matching variables incorrectly (false discoveries) decreased exponentially as more domains were added. Moving from 2 to 3 domains significantly reduced the standard deviation in the estimated number of shared nodes.\n  * Realistic Sparsity: The method successfully identified structures using \"partial pure children,\" a more relaxed and realistic assumption for biological systems than the \"pure children\" requirement found in prior work.\n\n## Insights and Future Directions\n\nThe most significant insight of this work is that fingerprints can replace pairs. If the underlying noise in a system is distinct enough, the joint distribution can be recovered without ever seeing the modalities simultaneously.\n\nHowever, the framework currently operates under several constraints:\n\n  * Linearity: The proofs and algorithms are currently limited to linear structural equations and linear mixing.\n  * Non-Gaussianity: The reliance on ICA means the method fails if the error distributions are Gaussian, as they lack the unique fingerprints necessary for matching.\n  * Observational Data: While the current work focuses on observational data, the authors suggest that incorporating interventional data (such as CRISPR knockouts) could further relax the requirements for sparsity and \"pure children.\"\n\nThis research provides a foundational theoretical step toward integrating disparate data types into a unified causal understanding of complex systems.\n\nBibTeX\n    \n    @inproceedings{sturma2023unpaired,\n      author = {Nils Sturma and Chandler Squires and Mathias Drton and Caroline Uhler},\n      title = {{Unpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning}},\n      booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems},\n      year = {2023},\n      url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=zW1uVN6Mbv},\n    }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Unpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning. (https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2302-00993.html)\nciteturn214search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # \"Unpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning.\"\n\n# \"Unpaired Multi-Domain Causal Representation Learning.\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nNils Sturma et al. (2023)\n\n  *   * Image: use mirror server\n\nDagstuhl\n\n  * Trier\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2302.00993\n\n\nmetadata version: 2024-08-04\n\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n\nauthority control:\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2302-00993\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Sc